      paragraphs: toParagraphs(`
The rain started at 2:11 a.m., thin as cassette tape and silver under the mag-rail, and Aya Sato nearly drove straight past the alley that changed the week. In Helix City, people still paid couriers in cash only when they were moving something that could not survive a camera. Aya had learned to respect that rule without loving it. So when a woman in a velvet coat slipped a paper envelope into her hand outside a shuttered jazz bar and said, "No scan, no copy, no questions," Aya tucked it under her jacket and promised herself she would make the drop, collect the credits, and forget the face by sunrise.

That plan lasted twelve blocks. Neon from the Old Loop bled across the flooded avenue in bruised pink and aquarium blue. Delivery drones skimmed the rooftops with their underlights off. Club kids stumbled laughing toward the all-night noodle windows. Somewhere above the street, on a stack of giant screens wrapped around a tower of mirrored apartments, Iona Vale's face flashed into emergency rotation. Helix loved Iona the way cities love their best liars: loudly, possessively, and only while the performance kept saving them from their own boredom. The alert under her frozen smile was simple enough to make Aya's stomach tighten. MISSING AFTER INCIDENT AT THE GLASS ARCADE.

Aya had never met Iona, but she had delivered flowers, costumes, and at least one illegal vocal synth patch to the singer's backstage doors over the last year. She knew the shape of Iona's security. She knew the bodyguards rotated in mirrored pairs and that the fans slept in shifts outside the theater on release nights. Most of all she knew that people like Iona did not disappear in the middle of a packed venue unless somebody had spent a great deal of money making normal rules stop applying. The envelope under Aya's jacket suddenly felt less like a delivery and more like a hook set between her ribs.

At a red light beneath the mag-rail, Aya checked the seal. No address. No sender. Just a hand-drawn crescent in silver ink, clean enough to look deliberate and messy enough to look personal. She had seen the same mark once before on a service door behind the Glass Arcade, half hidden by promo posters and fresh paint. She only remembered because a stage runner had touched the symbol on the way in and crossed himself like the old neighborhoods still did when they passed shrines or debt collectors. The light turned green. Aya did not move. Behind her, someone leaned on a horn. Ahead, the Arcade district glowed wet and expensive through the rain.

She told herself she was only cutting across the entertainment quarter because traffic would be lighter. That lie held until a patrol drone dipped low enough to read her courier plate and then, for reasons Aya did not understand, peeled away without logging her. The city rarely forgot to charge a working girl for existing. When it missed a chance, somebody richer than Aya was usually leaning on the system. She took the underpass route, skidding through spray and reflections, and tried not to think about how many different kinds of trouble could fit inside one ordinary-looking envelope.

At a street kitchen under the viaduct, old Mrs. Lian waved her over with a noodle ladle and told her the Old Loop had lost power for forty-seven seconds during Iona's encore. "Forty-seven is too long for an accident and too short for mercy," the woman said, spooning broth into a paper cup Aya had not asked for. A pair of teenagers nearby swore they had seen a woman in silver walk through a locked mirror backstage. A bouncer fresh from the Arcade said the singer's final line had cut out mid-note, but the speakers kept playing as if her voice were still standing there. Aya drank the broth in two burning gulps and hated that every rumor made her more curious.

By the time she reached the Glass Arcade, police tape fluttered across the front steps like party ribbon after a bad wedding. The crowd outside had not gone home. Fans pressed soaked flowers against the barricades. Streamers and gossipers kept trying to sell each other fake clips from inside the blackout. Aya left her bike beneath a dead ad screen, climbed a delivery scaffold she was definitely not supposed to know about, and slipped into the service lane behind the east wing. The silver crescent on the envelope felt warm through the paper now, as though somebody on the other side of the building had just touched its twin.

The backstage corridor beyond the service door was dark except for a single strip of emergency light that turned the mirrors red. Aya heard no voices, only a low feedback hum, patient and unpleasant, as if the building had a throat and was practicing someone else's song. At the end of the hall, the crescent symbol was painted fresh across a dressing-room door she knew should have led to costume storage. The paint was still wet. Beneath it, in a handwriting sharper than the one on the envelope, someone had scratched a message into the metal: If you came to deliver it, you are already late.

Aya should have walked out then. She knew it, the way people know a stove is hot before their hand touches it anyway. Instead she stood in the red hallway with rain dripping off her sleeves and felt the city narrowing around one bad choice. Helix always pretended every life could be redirected with enough speed and enough money. But sometimes a night crossed a line and everything after it belonged to the person who kept moving. Aya reached for the door handle because turning back suddenly felt more dangerous than stepping through.
      `),
